1
00:00:03,200 --> 00:00:06,392
Software quality is not just no bugs.

2
00:00:06,392 --> 00:00:13,384
It is conformance to the requirements, to development standards, and to what any professional would expect.

3
00:00:13,384 --> 00:00:26,222
The international standard ISO twenty five oh ten lists characteristics such as functional suitability, performance, usability, reliability, security, maintainability and portability.

4
00:00:27,433 --> 00:00:29,909
Quality is built in two ways.

5
00:00:29,909 --> 00:00:36,445
Quality assurance improves the process to prevent defects: standards, reviews and audits.

6
00:00:36,445 --> 00:00:39,661
Quality control tests the product to detect them.

7
00:00:39,661 --> 00:00:44,427
Similarly, verification asks: are we building the product right?

8
00:00:44,427 --> 00:00:47,597
Validation asks: are we building the right product?

9
00:00:48,800 --> 00:00:50,041
Two quality numbers.

10
00:00:50,267 --> 00:00:58,078
Availability is the mean time between failures over that plus the mean time to repair: ninety eight point seven seven per cent.

11
00:00:58,300 --> 00:01:04,108
Defect removal efficiency: ninety two of the hundred defects were caught before delivery.

12
00:01:04,333 --> 00:01:07,789
It sounds high, but it is four and a half days of downtime a year.

13
00:01:13,367 --> 00:01:14,866
The development models.

14
00:01:14,866 --> 00:01:24,762
Waterfall, the oldest, runs the phases once, strictly in order, each signed off before the next, like water falling down steps.

15
00:01:24,762 --> 00:01:28,759
It is simple, well documented and easy to manage.

16
00:01:28,759 --> 00:01:34,436
But the customer sees nothing until the end, and a changed requirement is very expensive.

17
00:01:34,436 --> 00:01:39,504
It suits short projects with a fixed specification, like a system defined by a law.

18
00:01:40,733 --> 00:01:50,201
The prototype model builds a quick, rough version, lets the user try it, and refines it, round and round, until the requirements are clear.

19
00:01:50,201 --> 00:02:01,523
The incremental model delivers the system in slices: a working core first, then fees, then attendance, then results, so the customer has something useful after a few weeks.

20
00:02:02,733 --> 00:02:05,599
Boehm's spiral model is driven by risk.

21
00:02:05,599 --> 00:02:15,886
Each loop passes through four quadrants: set objectives, identify and resolve risks with a prototype, develop and test, then plan the next loop.

22
00:02:15,886 --> 00:02:18,802
The spiral grows as cost accumulates.

23
00:02:18,802 --> 00:02:26,352
It suits large, expensive, safety critical systems, but it is costly and needs real risk expertise.

24
00:02:27,567 --> 00:02:32,972
Agile methods, like Scrum, follow the Agile Manifesto of two thousand and one.

25
00:02:32,972 --> 00:02:36,201
The product owner keeps a prioritised backlog.

26
00:02:36,201 --> 00:02:47,803
The team takes the top items into a sprint of one to four weeks, meets daily for fifteen minutes, and ends with a working, shippable increment, a review and a retrospective.

27
00:02:47,803 --> 00:02:51,188
Requirements may change freely between sprints.

28
00:02:51,188 --> 00:02:55,594
The cost: little documentation, and it needs an engaged customer.

29
00:02:56,800 --> 00:02:58,559
The models side by side.

30
00:02:58,559 --> 00:03:00,942
Fixed requirements: waterfall.

31
00:03:00,942 --> 00:03:03,195
Unclear ones: prototype.

32
00:03:03,195 --> 00:03:06,710
A system that splits into features: incremental.

33
00:03:06,710 --> 00:03:09,341
Large and dangerous: spiral.

34
00:03:09,341 --> 00:03:11,402
Constantly changing: agile.

35
00:03:12,633 --> 00:03:13,509
Planning sprints.

36
00:03:13,867 --> 00:03:19,617
Two sixty over forty is six and a half: seven sprints, twenty one weeks.

37
00:03:19,833 --> 00:03:23,414
Add sixty points: eight sprints, twenty four weeks.

38
00:03:23,633 --> 00:03:28,426
The change costs three weeks, and nothing already built is wasted.

39
00:03:28,426 --> 00:03:32,237
In waterfall it would mean redoing design, code and tests.

40
00:03:35,467 --> 00:03:36,237
Pause and think.

41
00:03:43,533 --> 00:03:48,578
The spiral model: each loop identifies and resolves the biggest remaining risk.

42
00:03:50,567 --> 00:03:51,002
One more.

43
00:03:58,633 --> 00:04:00,952
A process, named with a verb.

44
00:04:00,952 --> 00:04:03,209
DFDs have no decision symbol at all.

45
00:04:05,400 --> 00:04:06,508
Quick recap.

46
00:04:06,508 --> 00:04:09,217
Projects and their four constraints.

47
00:04:09,217 --> 00:04:12,107
The eight phases of the SDLC.

48
00:04:12,107 --> 00:04:14,777
Analysts, requirements and design.

49
00:04:14,777 --> 00:04:17,094
Data flow diagrams.

50
00:04:17,094 --> 00:04:18,540
Software quality.

51
00:04:18,540 --> 00:04:22,700
And the five development models, each suited to a different kind of project.

52
00:04:26,867 --> 00:04:29,082
That was the software process model.

53
